---
import site from '../../data/site.json';
---

<section class="sec sec-white" id="who">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="who-grid">
      <div class="who-vis reveal mob-who-video">
        <div
          class="who-box who-video"
          data-action="video-open"
          data-video-src={site.videoEmbedUrl}
          role="button"
          tabindex="0"
          aria-label="Watch our story"
        >
          <img
            src="/assets/images/team-photo.webp"
            alt="SIA Team"
            class="who-video-img"
            width="3839"
            height="2159"
          />
          <div class="who-video-shade"></div>
          <div class="who-video-play">
            <div class="who-video-btn">
              <svg width="28" height="28" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="#C8751A" aria-hidden="true"
                ><polygon points="6,3 20,12 6,21"></polygon></svg
              >
            </div>
            <span class="who-video-label">Watch Our Story</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="who-float"><span class="n">2011</span><span class="l">Founded</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="reveal d1 mob-who-text">
        <div class="sec-label">Who We Are</div>
        <h2 class="sec-title">Ireland's First<br />Archaeology School</h2>
        <div class="amber-bar"></div>
        <div class="who-text">
          <p>
            Imagine your child spending mid-term as a young archaeologist — trowel in hand, brushing
            soil from a real artefact, then shaping a witch's cauldron out of clay. That is what the
            SIA Halloween Camp is: active, hands-on learning that feels like a real adventure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Long before pumpkins and plastic costumes, Halloween was Samhain — and Ireland has been
            marking it for three thousand years. Over four days children dig, craft and step into
            that older story: the Cailleach, mummers' masks, Viking spears and lantern-lit folklore.
            Real history, real skills, and a mid-term they won't forget.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
